// The benchmark's corpus, compiled rather than generated in the browser.
//
// One CSV in, a GraphAr tree out: `fossil run` writes the vertex and edge Parquet and then runs the
// W3 layout pass over it, which fills `x` / `y` / `cluster_id` and rewrites each vertex file
// Morton-sorted. That last part is the whole reason this exists — a bbox query against a
// Morton-sorted Parquet prunes on row-group statistics, which is the larger-than-RAM half ADR-0001
// records as unmeasured.
//
// **One relation, one mapping, and that is forced rather than chosen.** The first draft declared
// `Node` twice — once over a node list for the attributes, once over an edge list for the links —
// which `fossil check` accepts and `fossil run` rejects:
//
//   UNION queries have different number of columns: left has 5 columns whereas right has 4
//
// Two mappings onto one vertex type are combined with a plain UNION ALL
// (`fossil-df/src/lib.rs`, `union(next.projected)`), so they must project identical schemas. An
// edge-list relation cannot match a node-list one, because neither has the other's column.
//
// So the source is denormalised: one row per edge, carrying the source node's own attributes. That
// is the ordinary RML shape — one logical source, one subject map, one predicate-object map.
//
// **It loses nodes, and by more than expected.** Measured at 2,000: 1,354 vertices written, a third
// of the corpus gone. The prediction was that isolated nodes would vanish; the truth is that any
// node which never appears as a *source* vanishes, because only the subject map mints a vertex and
// a target only ever supplies an IRI. A benchmark corpus that silently drops a third of its nodes
// is not a corpus, so this needs fixing before the numbers mean anything.
//
// Two shapes not yet tried: emit a row per node as well (target pointing at itself, and drop
// self-edges downstream), or give the endpoints their own mapping whose projection matches this
// one column for column.
//
// Two more things the first written corpus showed:
//   - `cluster_id` is 0 for every vertex. WCC is a reachability partition and this graph is
//     connected, so the giant component is the whole thing — which is precisely the case W3.2
//     (Leiden) exists to split, and until it lands `cluster_id` cannot colour anything here.
//   - the coordinates come out centred on the origin (−440..437), not in cosmos.gl's 0..4096 box.
//     That is not a defect: under ADR-0001 the source's space *is* the camera's, and the camera
//     fits to what it is given. It does mean nothing may rescale them on the way in.

prefix bench: <https://kanzo.tech/bench/>

rows := io.csv("rows.csv")

Node : bench:Node from rows
    iri = `${bench:}n/${.id}`
    bench:community = .community
    // An IRI-valued property is what an edge *is*: the endpoint merges with the vertex the
    // subject map produces for that same id, because dedup is by subject IRI.
    bench:linksTo = `${bench:}n/${.target}`
